{"qid":"Q5257","names":["Satavahana Dynasty"],"title":"Satavahana Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Satavahana dynasty, also called the Andhras in Puranic tradition, emerged in the Deccan around the late 2nd century BC and built the first major post-Mauryan empire of peninsular India, controlling the Krishna and Godavari deltas and extending at their apex across Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh, and into Madhya Pradesh. King Gautamiputra Satakarni (r. c. 78–102 CE) reversed Saka (Western Satrap) encroachments in the northwest, restoring Satavahana dominance over much of central India as attested in the Nashik cave inscriptions of his mother Gautami Balashri. The dynasty declined from the early 3rd century CE under pressure from a resurgent Western Satrap revival and internal fracture, finally dissolving around 220–230 CE when successor states — the Ikshvakus, Abhiras, and Vakatakas — absorbed its former territories.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is attested by epigraphy (Nashik, Nanaghat, Amaravati inscriptions) and coin distributions; Gautamiputra's claims in the Nashik inscription are the primary basis for maximum extent maps. Boundaries are approximate and scholarly consensus varies; moderate confidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Satavahana dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Satavahana-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Satavahana dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satavahana_dynasty"}],"britannica":"topic/Satavahana-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Satavahana dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Simuka","nativeName":"सिमुक","roma